! jb_ax5b "I-Box 5", routine 1268, TEST 1B and TEST 1C -- the deck's
! own test that an INSTRUCTION FETCHED CLOSE TO A STORE fetches the
! NEW instruction, run against INDEX STORAGE (1B) and against
! INTERNAL MEMORY (1C).  PDF pp.110-111, octal 024344.40-024363.00,
! 30 half-word rows, transcribed VERBATIM at the listing's own
! addresses and checked bit-exactly by
! verify_ax5b_1268_test1bc_listing.py.
!
! THIS BLOCK CLOSES A DOCUMENTED DEFERRAL.  The committed
! ax5b_1681_if_close_to_ds.strap stops with "A 'BEW,$' exit at
! 024344.40, where the listing continues into test 1B ... 1B and 1C
! need EX, deferred in docs/NEXT.md section 5."  024344.40 is exactly
! where this file starts.  EX is in the assembler (opcodes.py
! MISC_CODES "EX": 0b010) and already appears in three other
! transcriptions, so the deferral was only ever an encoding deferral
! and it is now closed on the encoding side.
!
! THE ROUTINE'S BANNER, verbatim, PDF p.110.  It prints between
! 024332.40 and 024333.00 and carries NO location of its own:
!
!     ----1268---IF AND DF CLOSE TO DS.
!
!               -THIS TEST IS COMPOSED OF TWO ROUTINES
!               -WHICH CHECK THE BELOW FUNCTIONS.
!
!                   -TEST 1 CHECKS IF CLOSE TO DS
!                    -IN ALL 3 MEMORIES.
!
!                   -TEST 2 CHECKS DF CLOSE TO DS
!                    -IN ALL 3 MEMORIES.
!
! The dash counts were MEASURED, not eyeballed: at 500 dpi the ink in
! the banner's dash band (full-res rows 422-432, page columns
! 1514-2058) forms two groups, 1514+44 / 1560+148 and 1911+48 /
! 1962+96, against a 48-pixel character pitch -- 194 px of ink before
! "1268" is FOUR dashes, 147 px after is THREE.  That is the same
! "----NNNN---" the other banners in this deck use (1244, 1246, 1250,
! 1258, 1262).  NOTE: the committed ax5b_1683_df_close_to_ds.strap
! quotes this banner as "----1268----IF AND DF CLOSE TO DS." with
! FOUR trailing dashes; by this measurement that is one dash too
! many.  Recorded, not corrected -- that file is not touched here.
!
! "IN ALL 3 MEMORIES" is the listing's own vocabulary and this file
! does not go past it.  The three are named by the three headings:
! EXT MEM (test 1A, already transcribed), IX STG and INT MEM (1B and
! 1C, here).  What the printed ADDRESS fields settle, and all they
! settle, is where each store lands: 1B stores to "$X3", which the
! assembler resolves to index register 3 = word 19 = octal 23, and
! the listing prints "23.03 10"; 1C stores to "$R", word 9 = octal
! 11 (opcodes.py SYSTEM_SYMBOLS "R": (9, 0) -- the right half of the
! accumulator; no page is cited for that here, the table is), and the
! listing prints
! "11.03 10".  Whether those two words live in physically distinct
! memories is NOT asserted here; it is the deck's word.
!
! HOW 1B AND 1C WORK, and why they need EX where 1A did not.  Test 1A
! plants a word over the location I682 and FALLS INTO it -- which
! only works for a location in the instruction stream, i.e. main
! (external) memory.  Index storage and word 9 are not in the
! instruction stream, so 1B and 1C reach them with EXECUTE instead:
!
!   LX,$X1,I68K1 / SX,$X1,$X3     plant [Z,$X2 | NOP] into word 19
!   NOP / NOP / NOP
!   LX,$X1,I68K2 / SX,$X1,$X3     plant [LVI,$X2,(8)777777.40 | NOP]
!   EX,$X3                        execute what is in word 19 NOW
!   LX,$X2,$X2                    re-read X2 to set the indicators
!   SIC,SEN / BXVZ,SERS           "-EXECUTED OLD INST FROM IX STG."
!
! Reference Manual printed p.49 (PDF p.52), "Execution Instructions":
! "One instruction, EXECUTE, directly specifies the location of a
! second instruction, the subject instruction, which is executed."
! So EX,$X3 fetches and executes the left half-word of word 19 --
! whatever the second SX has just put there.  If the fetch saw the
! second store, the LVI runs and X2 becomes 777777.40; if it got the
! stale first store, the Z runs and X2 is zero.
!
! WHY THE RE-READ.  Reference Manual printed p.43 (PDF p.46), Figure
! 11 Indicator List, row 50: "XVZ ... Index Value Zero", class T,C,
! braced with 48/49/51 under "Reset by index arithmetic ops. other
! than comparisons".  "LX,$X2,$X2" reads X2 from its own word,
! changing nothing, purely so that index-arithmetic reset fires and
! the value indicators describe X2 -- which is what the following
! BXVZ tests.  Test 1A does the identical thing for the identical
! reason.
!
! BRANCH POLARITY: BRANCH-TAKEN IS THE ERROR PATH.  These rows use
! the MINORITY shape of the two in this deck -- "SIC,SEN" first, then
! "BXVZ,SERS", with nothing branching to an OK label.  B<IND>
! branches when the indicator is ON, so BXVZ branches when Index
! Value Zero is on, i.e. when X2 came out ZERO, i.e. when the STALE
! "Z,$X2" ran.  Branch taken -> SERS -> the message.  The message
! therefore names what went wrong, and the straight-line fall-through
! is the pass.  Same shape as routine 1274, not the "branch to OK"
! shape most 160/162 blocks use.
!
! NO LABEL IS DEFINED IN THIS RANGE.  The listing's label column is
! empty for all 30 rows: I681 is at 024337.00 (above), I683 at
! 024363.40 (below), and everything between is unlabelled.  So every
! address in this block is either self-relative or external, and the
! witness tiering in verify_ax5b_1268_test1bc_listing.py has no
! "label placed by the SLC" to argue about.
!
! GLYPHS.  Every label here is spelled with a LETTER I -- I681, I68K1,
! I68K2, IC268 -- and "SEN0" with a DIGIT zero.  When this file was
! written the letter I was house style for IC268 only, carried by the
! IC244/IC246/IC258/IC260/IC262/IC274 spellings already in programs/
! and verify_*.py, while I681 and I68K1/2 were written 1681 and
! 168K1/2 with an "L" prefixed to make them legal for this assembler.
! That reading has since been measured and is wrong: the leading glyph
! is the letter throughout (docs/listing_typography.md), and the
! names are now uniform.  The digit zero of SEN0 keeps the pixel
! measurement ax5b_1274_truncation.strap records for the
! "SIC,SEN0+.32" row ("has digit 0 at 45 px").  "1B"/"1C"/"1268"
! remain digits -- they are the deck's test and routine NUMBERS, not
! labels, and the rename left routine numbers decimal.
!
! SUBSTITUTIONS, three, and a fourth that has been retired:
!   1. RETIRED.  This item used to read "numeric labels are prefixed
!      L (L1681, L168K1, L168K2, IC268); the listing writes them
!      bare".  It came from the misreading the GLYPHS note above
!      records, and there is no label substitution left: I681, I68K1,
!      I68K2 and IC268 are written as the listing prints them.
!   2. ENT1B is this file's own name for the first row.  The listing
!      prints NO label there; STRAP's END needs one.
!   3. SERS is a "B,$" HANG at its own printed address 001304.00, and
!      SSW likewise at 001301.00; the real error-report and
!      switch-scan routines are SSIP's and are not transcribed.
!   4. A "BEW,$" exit at 024363.40, where the listing continues into
!      routine 1268's test 2A -- already transcribed as
!      ax5b_1683_df_close_to_ds.strap.
! I68K1 and I68K2 are placed from PDF p.111 at their printed
! addresses, not manufactured; they are the same two words test 1A
! plants, and they are re-placed here only so the block could be run.
! Everything else is verbatim.
!
! NOT RUN.  This session was instructed not to invoke run_program.sh,
! run_tests.sh, bsc or mutation_score.py, so this file is an ENCODING
! oracle only and is NOT wired into run_tests.sh.  The substitution
! blocks above exist so a follow-up can wire it in; they are
! UNVERIFIED as an execution.  Nothing in this header claims anything
! about what the model does at run time.
!
! THIS BLOCK DOES NOT BEAR ON THE ADDRESS-ALARM INDICATORS.  "DF" and
! "DS" in the routine's title are the Data Fetch and Data Store
! PATHS, not indicators 20 and 19.  Nothing in these 30 rows writes
! storage location 3 (the boundary registers), touches the mask,
! names a protected address, or branches on DF/DS/IF; the only
! indicator branch is BXVZ, indicator 50.  Reference Manual printed
! p.32 (PDF p.35): "The three address alarm indicators, DS, DF, and
! IF, are permanent.  Once turned on, they remain on until they cause
! an interruption or are turned off by program control."  That is a
! different mechanism from anything here, and this block independently
! corroborates the reading ax5b_1683_df_close_to_ds.strap arrived at.

! ---- externals: INPUTS to the check, never witnesses ---------------
I68K1  SYN, (8)24407.0     ! p.111 label column: I68K1  [Z,$X2 | NOP]
I68K2  SYN, (8)24410.0     ! p.111 label column: I68K2  [LVI.. | NOP]
I681   SYN, (8)24337.0     ! p.110 label column: I681, the loop head
IC268  SYN, (8)24405.0     ! p.111 label column: IC268, continuity reg
BIT0    SYN, (8)24522.0     ! shared pool; NOT printed on pp.110-111
SEN     SYN, (8)1310.0      ! SSIP's, per p.116's SYN table
SEN0    SYN, (8)1311.0      ! SSIP's
SERS    SYN, (8)1304.0      ! SSIP's
SSW     SYN, (8)1301.0      ! SSIP's

        SLC, (8)24344.40
!
! ---- TEST 1B: store into INDEX STORAGE, then EXECUTE it ------------
ENT1B   LX,  $X1, I68K1           ! -TEST 1B, IF CLOSE TO DS IN IX STG.
        SX,  $X1, $X3              !  plant the OLD instruction in word 19
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        LX,  $X1, I68K2
        SX,  $X1, $X3              !  plant the NEW one over it
        EX,  $X3                   !  execute what is in word 19 NOW
        LX,  $X2, $X2              !  re-read X2 to set the indicators
        SIC, SEN
        BXVZ, SERS                 ! -EXECUTED OLD INST FROM IX STG.
!
! ---- TEST 1C: store into INTERNAL MEMORY (word 9), then EXECUTE ----
        LX,  $X1, I68K1           ! -TEST 1C, IF CLOSE TO DS IN INT MEM.
        SX,  $X1, $R               !  plant the OLD instruction in word 9
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        LX,  $X1, I68K2
        SX,  $X1, $R               !  plant the NEW one over it
        EX,  $R                    !  execute what is in word 9 NOW
        LX,  $X2, $X2              !  re-read X2 to set the indicators
        SIC, SEN
        BXVZ, SERS                 ! -EXECUTED OLD INST FROM INT MEM.
!
! ---- loop once, return to SSIP -------------------------------------
        B,   $+1.0                 !  skip the BD: -> 024360.40
        BD,  I681                 !  the loop back to I681
        SIC, SEN0+.32
        B,   SSW                   ! -TO SSIP.
        BD,  $+.32
!
! ---- the routine's continuity register, test 1's bit ---------------
        LX,  $X13, IC268          ! -UPDATE CONTINUITY CHECK.
        V+,  $X13, BIT0            !  test 1 done = bit 0
        SX,  $X13, IC268
!
! ---- substitution 4: the exit --------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)24363.40
PASS    BEW, $
!
! ---- substitution: the two planted words, from p.111 ---------------
        SLC, (8)24407.0
K1      Z,   $X2                   ! I68K1: the OLD instruction
        NOP
        SLC, (8)24410.0
K2      LVI, $X2, (8)777777.40     ! I68K2: the NEW one
        NOP
!
! ---- substitution: the continuity register's own word --------------
        SLC, (8)24405.0
CREG    XW,  0, 0, 0               ! -CONTINUITY REG 1268.
!
        SLC, (8)1310.0
SENW    DD(BU,64), 0               ! SIC's target
! ---- substitution 3: SERS and SSW are hangs ------------------------
        SLC, (8)1301.0
SSWH    B, $
        SLC, (8)1304.0
SERSH   B, $
        END, ENT1B
